on/math-academy-id "q-94005" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} & \\,∗\\, \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} & \\,∗\\,\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the second column must be orthogonal to the first column, and\n\n- the norm of the second column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357621 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172025 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6598e4e-7ea6-56ce-abef-95e64d010e96" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parts of an Orthogonal Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04f5183c-a1e7-52fc-a81b-7d2fba7a799b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12}{13} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{13}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354694} {:answer/id #uuid "846cae1c-da01-5d91-ad29-413f58d07fde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{13} \\\\ -\\frac{12}{13}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355832} {:answer/id #uuid "c1cb7d2a-602c-5418-819c-d477541b4439" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 175921860454